  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 221 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Breezes will remain southerly today and \ntomorrow, but will be increasing. Gusts 15 to 25 mph can be \nexpected today, especially in the northern Champlain Valley and \nSt. Lawrence Valley. Wednesday will see a surge of warm air \nadvection as southerly flow deepens and the pressure gradient \ntightens. Gusts to around 30 mph are probable for channeled \nlocations with 15 to 20 mph gusts expected elsewhere.\nTemperatures  will sharply rise over today's highs in the \nlow/mid 60s to the upper 60s to mid 70s by Wednesday afternoon.\nAs the front and rainfall moves in Thursday, temperatures will\nbegin to trend cooler with highs back in the 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Light radar returns are evident across the \nCanadian border with stronger returns evident in the vicinity of\nLake Huron. These returns will track eastward through the day as\na weak wave moves along the northern periphery of high pressure\ncentered over the mid Atlantic states. Best chances for rain\nremain towards the Canadian border this afternoon and overnight\nwith only a few hundredths to around 0.12\" of rain. \n\nThe next chances of rain will be on Thursday as the front moves\ninto the region. Models are showing more of a consensus that the\nonset of precipitation will be earlier than previous forecasts\nbeginning in the early morning hours across northern New York\nand by daybreak in Vermont. The biggest change has been in QPF\nwhich has sharply lowered as strongest forcing shifts south of\nthe region. Chances are decreasing on amounts and most likely\namounts falling below 0.25\" Thursday. There are still a few\nensemble members that have more forcing farther north, but those\nhave been dwindling each run. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: At least a couple of chances for showers remains\non track Friday through Monday; however, uncertainty in timing \nis large. As of now, the most likely scenario for precipitation \nforcing relates to a wave of low pressure developing to our \nsoutheast by Friday evening and working its way northeastward \nSaturday, while a trailing upper level low slowly crosses our \nregion from the west. The potential for rainfall exceeding an \ninch has decreased with the latest ensemble guidance, as fewer \nmembers are supporting the idea of this low track retrograding \nto the northwest quickly enough to bring a conveyor belt of \nheavier rain this far north and west (as opposed to eastern New \nEngland). Instead, much of the rain over the weekend may be tied\nsimply to showers that swing through in cyclonic, upslope flow.\n\nIn general, the GEFS supports a faster progression of the upper \nlevel trough with weaker downstream blocking compared to the ENS, \nsupporting a Friday night into Saturday timing as opposed to showers \ncoming through later in the weekend into Monday. While there is \nplenty of uncertainty in the details, the shift to troughing will \nlead to a cooler and unsettled weather pattern into early next week \neven if there are extended dry periods for a given location. For \nthose looking forward to winter weather, temperatures aloft may cool \nenough for mountain snow chances, with about a 30-50% chance snow \nlevels fall to 3000 feet Sunday night into Monday.",
